

# Spring Transactions

## Notes

1. What is a transaction?
   Think of it like you send 1000 rs to your friend and your friend receives it.
   This is a transaction.
   Now again you send 1k but this time your friend has not received,
   and the money is also not credited to your account. Means it’s completely gone . to where no one knows.
   So, the transaction ensures either all operations are successful, or none of them happen.
   Without transaction db becomes inconsistent.

   Spring provides @Transactional annotation to automatically manage db transactions.

2. How Transactional works?
   @Transactional is a Spring annotation used to manage database transactions declaratively.
   When a method annotated with @Transactional is called, Spring creates an AOP proxy around the bean.
   The proxy starts a transaction before the method executes and either commits it if the method completes successfully
   or rolls it back if a runtime exception occurs.
   This ensures the ACID properties of transactions and keeps the database consistent.
   We typically place @Transactional on service-layer methods because a single business operation often involves
   multiple repository calls that should succeed or fail together.

3. Why use @Transactional?
   Because it automatically manages transactions, ensuring all database operations either complete successfully together
   or are rolled back together, maintaining data consistency.

4. Where should @Transactional be placed?
   Prefer the Service layer, because a business operation may involve multiple repository calls that should succeed or
   fail as one unit.

5. Why not in Repository?
   A repository usually performs a single database operation. Business logic often spans multiple repositories,
   so transaction boundaries belong in the service layer.

6. Why does Spring use proxies?
   Spring uses proxies to intercept method calls and apply additional behavior such as transaction management, logging,
   security, caching, or asynchronous execution without modifying the original business logic.

7. Does Spring modify my original class?
   No. Spring creates a proxy object around the original bean.
   The proxy method calls, executes additional logic, and then delegates the call to the original object it intercepts .

8. Why doesn't @Transactional work on self-invocation?
   Because a method calling another method in the same class is a normal Java call.
   It bypasses the Spring proxy,
   so the transactional interceptor never gets a chance to start a transaction.
   Spring AOP is a programming approach that lets us apply cross-cutting concerns such as transaction management,
   logging, security, caching, and auditing without changing the business logic. Spring implements AOP using proxies.
   Instead of the client calling the target object directly, it calls a proxy object.
   The proxy intercepts the method call, executes additional logic like starting a transaction or logging,
   delegates the call to the target method, and then performs post-processing such as commit or rollback.
   Spring uses JDK Dynamic Proxies when the bean implements an interface, and CGLIB proxies when it doesn't.

   - begin transaction
   - commit
   - rollback

   Spring intercepts your method call.
   Think of the proxy as a security guard.

9. What is a Proxy?
   A proxy is simply another object that stands in front of the original object.

10. What is the default propagation?
   Propagation.REQUIRED.
   It joins an existing transaction if one is present; otherwise, it starts a new transaction.

11. Difference between REQUIRED and REQUIRES_NEW?
    REQUIRED participates in the current transaction if one exists. REQUIRES_NEW always suspends the current transaction (if any) and starts a completely new, independent transaction.

12. When does Spring roll back a transaction?

   By default, Spring rolls back on RuntimeException and Error. Checked exceptions do not trigger rollback unless configured using rollbackFor.
   Why does @Transactional fail when a method calls another transactional method within the same class? How do you fix it?

   @Transactional works through Spring AOP proxies. The proxy intercepts method calls, starts a transaction before the method executes, and commits or rolls back the transaction afterward. When one method in the same class directly calls another method, it's a normal Java method call (this.method()), so the call bypasses the Spring proxy. Since the proxy is not involved, Spring never gets a chance to start the transaction, and the @Transactional annotation is ignored. To fix this, we should ensure the call goes through the Spring proxy, typically by moving the transactional method to another Spring-managed bean or by obtaining the proxied bean instead of calling the method directly.

13.Will private methods work with @Transactional?
   No,
   The proxy cannot intercept private methods because they cannot be overridden.

14.Will final methods work?
   With CGLIB proxies, no.
   CGLIB creates a subclass and overrides methods.
   A final method cannot be overridden.
   So the proxy cannot add transactional behavior.

   Transaction propagation defines how a transactional method should behave when it is called from another
   transactional method.
   It determines whether the method should join the existing transaction, create a new transaction,
   suspend the existing transaction, or execute without a transaction.

   The default is REQUIRED, which joins the existing transaction if one exists, otherwise creates a new one.

   REQUIRES_NEW always creates an independent transaction by suspending the current one.

   REQUIRED- Joins an existing transaction or creates if none exists

    REQUIRES_NEW - suspends if any existing transaction exists and creates a complete new, independent transaction

    SUPPORTS- Use the existing transaction if there is one; otherwise run without a transaction.

    MANDATORY- A transaction must already exist.

    NOT_SUPPORTED -Don't run inside a transaction.If a transaction already exists, Spring suspends it temporarily.

    NEVER-This method must not run inside a transaction.

    NESTED -Run inside the existing transaction using a savepoint.


15.What is the "Self-Invocation" trap, and how do you resolve it?
   Self-invocation occurs when a method in a Spring bean calls another method of the same bean directly.
   Since the call is made directly using this, it bypasses the Spring AOP proxy. Because @Transactional is applied
   through the proxy, the transactional advice on the called method is not executed. The preferred solution is to move
   the transactional method to another Spring-managed bean and call it through that bean, ensuring the call passes
   through the proxy. Another option is to obtain the proxied instance, but separating the responsibility into another
   service is generally cleaner.

16.How does Spring decide which TransactionManager to use?
   When @Transactional is used, Spring's transaction interceptor delegates transaction management to a
   PlatformTransactionManager. If a transaction manager is explicitly specified using the transactionManager attribute,
   Spring uses that bean. Otherwise, Spring resolves an appropriate transaction manager from the application context.
   In a typical Spring Boot application with a single JPA datasource, Boot auto-configures a JpaTransactionManager,
   so @Transactional can use it by default. If multiple transaction managers exist, we can use @Primary to define the
   default or explicitly specify the required transaction manager in @Transactional

17.How does spring @Transactional rollback work internally? And common pitfalls

   @Transactional is implemented through Spring's AOP proxy. When a transactional method is called through the proxy,
   the transaction interceptor starts a transaction. If the method completes successfully, the transaction is committed.
   If an exception is thrown, Spring evaluates its rollback rules. By default, it rolls back for RuntimeException and
   Error, but not checked exceptions. We can customize this using rollbackFor and noRollbackFor.
   Common pitfalls include self-invocation, because it bypasses the proxy; catching an exception inside
   the transactional method, which can prevent Spring from detecting the failure; expecting checked exceptions
   to automatically cause rollback; putting @Transactional on private methods; keeping transactions open during slow
   external calls; and assuming a database rollback can undo an external API operation.

  Also, REQUIRES_NEW creates an independent transaction, and @Transactional doesn't provide a distributed transaction
  across microservices."


